# 2026-08-10 split: casing noise vs. a real field-prefix-on-a-local signal.
#
# EXEMPT: 1-character loop counters. `i` and `j` are universal, and PascalCasing
# them yields `I`/`J` -- pure churn. On drag-lint's own source this shape alone
# was 135 of 195 local-var-casing findings, 116 of them the single name `i`.
# The absence assertions below are the load-bearing half of this fixture: without
# them, a rule that simply stopped firing would still pass.
local-var-casing 26
local-var-casing 27

# `fi` (line 26) is the case that proves the exemption is about LENGTH, not about
# being a loop counter -- it IS one, and it still fires, because at 2 characters
# a reader gains something from the fix. It is also the exact name whose autofix
# proposed `Fi`, which would have invented a fake field prefix; that is why the
# casing fix and the prefix rule had to stop sharing one message.

# A local wearing the F field prefix is its own rule now, and it is the signal
# worth acting on: F is reserved for object fields, so `FName` in a routine body
# reads as a field and is not one.
local-field-prefix 28
local-field-prefix 29

# CONTROLS -- these keep the split honest.
# GLE (line 30) is a short all-caps initialism, already exempt from casing.
# Good (line 31) is clean.
# Neither may draw either rule, and no F-prefixed local may be reported as a
# casing violation any more (that conflation is what the split removes).
!hungarian-or-short-identifier
